The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
// in ni_usb_setup_init():
if (i > NUM_INIT_WRITES) {
    dev_err(&usb_dev->dev, "bug!, buffer overrun, i=%i\n", i);
    return 0;
}

// in ni_usb_init():
if (writes_len)
    retval = ni_usb_write_registers(ni_priv, writes, writes_len, &ibsta);
else
    return -EFAULT;
```
Reason:
When `ni_usb_setup_init()` detects a buffer overrun, it erroneously returns `0` (success) instead of a negative error code. This causes `ni_usb_init()` to treat the call as successful (`writes_len` is 0, so the `if` condition fails) and enter the `else` branch, where it returns `-EFAULT` without freeing the allocated `writes` buffer, resulting in a memory leak. Additionally, the error code `-EFAULT` is inappropriate for this situation.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
// in ni_usb_setup_init():
if (i > NUM_INIT_WRITES) {
    dev_err(&usb_dev->dev, "bug!, buffer overrun, i=%i\n", i);
    return -EINVAL;
}

// in ni_usb_init():
if (writes_len < 0) {
    kfree(writes);
    return writes_len;
}
```
Reason:
Returning `-EINVAL` from `ni_usb_setup_init()` on buffer overrun provides a proper error code. In `ni_usb_init()`, checking `writes_len < 0` correctly identifies failure, frees the allocated `writes` buffer to prevent the memory leak, and propagates the exact error code from `ni_usb_setup_init()`. The unconditional `kfree(writes);` later in the function handles the success case.

